{
  "version": "0.2.1",
  "note": "Compiled from BADAN_MASTER_BLUEPRINT.md by scripts/mine-blueprint workflow. Source of truth for the app registry. (§10 Health metric ontology) v0.2.0 (EPIC-07) adds the ingested workout.* family, wrist-temperature deviation, the Phase-2 derived score ids, and the v1.1 domain ids (hydration/cycle/lab) per plan §28.1 amendment 12, so no second registry pass is needed. Four ids from the EPIC-07 brief were NOT added because an existing row already means the same thing and duplicating it would split one fact across two ids: heart.hrv_sdnn (heart.hrv is the SDNN-fed row — its preciseLabel said rMSSD and was corrected in v0.2.0), respiratory.rr (respiratory.rate), activity.basal_energy (activity.resting_energy), body.vo2max (heart.vo2max). Every canonicalUnit and displayUnit here must exist in Units.table on both platforms — UnitTests.testRegistryCanonicalAndDisplayUnitsAreAllKnown enforces it. Families beyond MetricFamily's cases (workout/readiness/effort/stress/hydration/cycle/lab) intentionally fall back to .other in the UI until those cases are added; the registry is designed to add families without a code change. v0.2.1 (EPIC-29) adds one row: journal.correlation. That row is a NAMESPACE, not a single measured metric — the correlation engine writes journal.correlation.<behaviorId>.<scoreId>, one id per behaviour x score pair the user has logged enough of, and those concrete ids are deliberately not enumerated here (the set is a product of behaviors.json and the three scored ids, so listing it would add ~90 catalogue rows that never carry data and would rot the moment a behaviour is added). The namespace row is what gives the whole family its unit, chart hint, bilingual name and — load-bearing — its sensitiveClass: sensitivePlus, because a finding names the behaviour it is about (caffeine, alcohol) and is therefore as lifestyle-revealing as the journal row it came from (I7). Anything resolving a journal.correlation.* id falls back to this row for that identity.",
